{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795202338591,"sessionID":"<redacted>","part":{"partID":"prt_1fa65cd09002Oi2hwpxn151na3","sessionID":"<redacted>","messageID":"msg_1fa65c31a001AuqXV82k0v9t5l","type":"tool","id":"call_dd2cace38ff555e68f1d7483","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== dsh sandbox docs ===\"; grep -rn \"danger-full-access\" .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-presentation.txt .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research-presentation.txt .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog/executor/executor-config.md .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md | head -20\necho \"=== read-only convergence ===\"; grep -rn \"read-only\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog/script-entry-points/convergence-script.md .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/convergence/convergence.md | head -12\necho \"=== identity docs ===\"; grep -rn \"idempotency\\|claimedTimestamp\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog/script-entry-points/append-mode-event-script.md .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/state/state-jsonl.md | head -12"},"output":"=== dsh sandbox docs ===\n.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-presentation.txt:142:   |-- --sandbox-mode=<mode> -> config.executor.sandboxMode (`read-only` / `workspace-write` / `danger-full-access`; default `workspace-write`; cli-opencode also defaults to `workspace-write`, which denies its file tools outside the working directory but not Bash; `read-only` is refused)\n.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-presentation.txt:222:      C) cli-claude-code — `claude -p \"PROMPT\" --model X --permission-mode X`, where the permission mode is `acceptEdits` at the default `workspace-write` and `bypassPermissions` with `--sandbox-mode=danger-full-access`. The default cannot run Bash in `-p` mode, so a lineage that must record state needs the flag. Optional --effort and optional `--config-dir=PATH` for CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. No service-tier.\n.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research-presentation.txt:131:   |-- --sandbox-mode=<mode> -> config.executor.sandboxMode (`read-only` / `workspace-write` / `danger-full-access`; default `workspace-write`; cli-opencode also defaults to `workspace-write`, which denies its file tools outside the working directory but not Bash; `read-only` is refused)\n.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research-presentation.txt:195:     C) cli-claude-code — `claude -p \"PROMPT\" --model X --permission-mode X`, where the permission mode is `acceptEdits` at the default `workspace-write` and `bypassPermissions` with `--sandbox-mode=danger-full-access`; the default cannot run Bash in `-p` mode, so a lineage that must record state needs the flag. Optional --effort and optional `--config-dir=PATH` for CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. No service-tier.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md:286:- `cli-opencode`: pass the rendered prompt as the last argument of the command `buildLineageCommand` in `fanout-run.cjs` builds, `opencode run --standalone -m X[#Y] --format json --auto \"PROMPT\"`, started in `{repo_root}` with stdin closed. `reasoningEffort` Y rides as the `#Y` suffix on the model. `--auto` is always passed. `dispatch-iteration.cjs` resolves an unset sandbox mode to `workspace-write`, as fan-out does, and under it the builder hands the child an inline `OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT` that denies `external_directory`; the receipt records `sandboxMode` `workspace-write`. That deny stops OpenCode's file tools at the working directory but does not confine Bash. **In both modes that run, Bash keeps full OS-level write access.** `danger-full-access` adds no deny, and OpenCode exposes no confinement flag, so an explicit `read-only` fails closed before dispatch. The real containment is (a) the `external_directory` deny on the file tools under `workspace-write`, (b) the prompt-level \"ALLOWED WRITE PATHS\" / \"BANNED OPERATIONS\" contract rendered into the iteration prompt (`assets/prompt-pack-iteration.md.tmpl`), which relies on the model obeying instructions rather than an OS-level sandbox, and (c) post-dispatch validation (`validateIterationOutputs`) catching some violations after the fact. Because review targets are arbitrary code/spec files, they MUST be treated as potentially adversarial content (untrusted) for prompt-injection purposes — the same containment posture as untrusted fetched content in deep-research.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md:287:- `cli-claude-code`: `claude -p \"$(cat prompt)\" --model X --permission-mode <mode> --output-format text` with optional `--effort Y`. The permission mode resolves from `--sandbox-mode`: unset or `workspace-write` runs `acceptEdits` so iteration writes succeed, `read-only` runs `plan` and `danger-full-access` runs `bypassPermissions`. The inline workflow branches are not the complete schema: `cli-codex`, `cli-cursor`, `cli-devin`, `cli-pi` and `cli-deepseek-harness` (experimental) also have inline branches that run `dispatch-iteration.cjs`, and `cli-mistral-vibe` runs only as a fan-out lineage. The executor-kind authority is `runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts` (`EXECUTOR_KINDS`).\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md:288:- Sandbox on those `dispatch-iteration.cjs` branches: `cli-codex`, `cli-cursor`, `cli-devin`, `cli-pi` and `cli-deepseek-harness` resolve the requested `--sandbox-mode` and map it to the kind's own read-only or write surface, which is an OS sandbox only on codex, cursor and the deepseek harness. An unset mode resolves to `workspace-write`, so the iteration can write its iteration file, delta and state record. A requested `read-only` is passed through, never widened, and the iteration then fails at the post-dispatch artifact gate rather than gaining write access. The deepseek harness narrows `danger-full-access` to `workspace-write`, and pi builds the same command for both write modes.\n=== read-only convergence ===\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog/script-entry-points/convergence-script.md:61:`--record-blocked-stop` makes the command record a blocked research or review stop itself, so a leaf that reads a BLOCKED decision and skips the recording step still leaves a state row. It needs `--stop-decision` and `--iteration` as a positive integer, and it is refused with `--read-only`; each refusal is an input error. When the decision is BLOCKED, the command appends the record through `append-mode-event.cjs`, whether the graph has nodes, is empty or cannot be read. For research it builds the `blocked_stop` event row the research workflow sends, and the row's `recoveryStrategy` names, when it applies, the iterations that recorded findings but left no FINDING node in the graph, the usual sign of a graph upsert that never ran. For review it builds the `deep_review.blocked_stop_recorded` event: scope from the config `sessionId` and `generation`, one gate row per legal-stop gate plus one fail row per graph blocker, finding counts from the registry, `recoveryStrategy` as the first blocked gate in kebab form, and `targetDimensionId` as the dimension the next iteration is steered to, or `all-dimensions-covered` when none is left. The payload gains `blocked_stop_record` with `status` `recorded` (plus `eventId` when the gateway returns a receipt), `already_recorded` when the state log already holds a row for this run with the same blocker set, or `failed` with the gateway's `exitCode` and `reason`; a review record also carries `recoveryHint` and `gateResults`. A changed blocker set is a new attempt and gets its own row. A failed record exits with the gateway's code, after the decision is printed.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog/script-entry-points/convergence-script.md:81:| `tests/integration/convergence-blocked-stop.vitest.ts` | Integration | `--record-blocked-stop`: one row per run and blocker set, a new row for a changed blocker set, a failed command when the gateway refuses, nothing without the flag, the `--read-only` refusal, and the iterations named for a missing graph upsert. |\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/convergence/convergence.md:101:| `divergent` | After all review gates pass, translates the eligible `all_dimensions_clean` STOP into a read-only three-seat direction-selection pivot and continues with the selected review direction. |\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/convergence/convergence.md:140:`convergence.cjs` records the following row in `deep-review-state.jsonl` whenever the legal-stop decision tree vetoes STOP. `step_graph_convergence` in `deep-review.yaml` runs it with `--record-blocked-stop` (live run only; a dry run passes `--read-only` instead, so it appends no blocked-stop row, persists no snapshot and writes no observability event), and on a BLOCKED decision it appends a `deep_review.blocked_stop_recorded` event through the append gateway, which projects to this row. The workflow never writes it by hand. The gate names and their shapes are load-bearing, the reducer reads them verbatim:\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/convergence/convergence.md:456:> **GATE MODEL, two naming conventions mapped to one authoritative set.** The authoritative producer is `convergence.cjs`, which the `step_graph_convergence` step in `deep-review.yaml` runs with `--record-blocked-stop` (live run only; a dry run passes `--read-only` instead, so it appends no blocked-stop row, persists no snapshot and writes no observability event) and which records **9 gates** with the `Gate` suffix: `convergenceGate` / `dimensionCoverageGate` / `p0ResolutionGate` / `evidenceDensityGate` / `hotspotSaturationGate` / `claimAdjudicationGate` / `fixCompletenessReplayGate` / `candidateCoverageGate` / `graphlessFallbackGate`. The §1 event shape mirrors this set verbatim and the reducer reads those names verbatim, so treat the event shape as authoritative when writing or reading JSONL state. The §6 table below is the high-level conceptual model and uses descriptive names WITHOUT the `Gate` suffix where one reads more naturally (for example `findingStability` maps to `convergenceGate`); each row carries its event-shape name so the mapping is explicit. `candidateCoverageGate` and `graphlessFallbackGate` are v2-rollout gates that pass trivially when the review-depth-v2 search path is inactive. The gate-model drift cluster (LG-0013, LG-0016, LG-0031, LG-0032) has been reconciled.\n=== identity docs ===\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog/script-entry-points/append-mode-event-script.md:29:A legacy research row or legacy review row (`type` of `iteration`, `iteration_start` or `event`), the shape a model writes, does not choose its own identity. The gateway drops the row's `eventId`, `idempotencyKey`, `producer` and `authorityEpoch` (and their snake_case forms) and mints all four: an `event-<uuid>` id, the key `deep-research:input:<sha256>` derived from the row as sent, producer `deep-loop-cli` and the admission epoch. None of the four is kept as a claim, because `claimedTimestamp` is the only claim field. A row that reused an earlier row's key or id therefore cannot pass as its retry or collide with it, and an identical row appended twice is still stored once. A row with a `stem` or an `event_type`, and a canonical envelope, still keep the identity the caller names.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog/script-entry-points/append-mode-event-script.md:33:A model writes the iteration row, so the time on it is a claim, not the row time. The gateway times every research iteration row (`deep_research.iteration_completed`) and every review `\"type\":\"iteration\"` record from its own clock, and ignores an `occurredAt` or `occurred_at` the row carries. The stamp the model wrote is kept as a claim. A research row's UTC `timestamp` moves to `claimedTimestamp` in the event data. A review record is stored whole, its own `timestamp` included. For both, the projected state-log row carries the gateway time as `timestamp` and the model's stamp as `claimedTimestamp`. A research stamp that is not a UTC instant is dropped with a warning. Every other row keeps its own timestamp.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog/script-entry-points/append-mode-event-script.md:52:| `tests/unit/append-mode-event-legacy-seam.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Research iteration rows timed by the gateway clock, with the model's stamp kept as `claimedTimestamp`; a model-written research or review row's event id, idempotency key, producer and epoch minted by the gateway, an identical model-written row appended twice stored once, and a reused key with other content refused as `IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT`. |\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog/script-entry-points/append-mode-event-script.md:53:| `tests/unit/append-mode-event-cli.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Review iteration records timed by the gateway clock, with the model's stamp kept as `claimedTimestamp`. |\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/state/state-jsonl.md:97:**Row time:** the review agent writes `timestamp`, but the append gateway times an iteration record by its own clock. Any time field on the record, `occurredAt` included, never sets the ledger event time. The ledger projection writes the event time as `timestamp` and keeps the record's own stamp as `claimedTimestamp`, so a wrong or future stamp stays visible without timing the row. Only `type: \"iteration\"` records are timed this way. The workflow's own rows, such as `iteration_start` and the dispatch and containment event rows, keep the `timestamp` their writer gave them.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/state/state-jsonl.md:101:**Optional fields:** `parentSessionId`, `continuedFromRun`, `findingsRefined`, `findingRefs`, `traceabilityChecks`, `coverage`, `noveltyJustification`, `ruledOut`, `focusTrack`, `scoreEstimate`, `segment`, `convergenceSignals`, `graphEvents`, `claimedTimestamp` (written by the ledger projection, never by the agent)\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== dsh sandbox docs ===\n.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-presentation.txt:142:   |-- --sandbox-mode=<mode> -> config.executor.sandboxMode (`read-only` / `workspace-write` / `danger-full-access`; default `workspace-write`; cli-opencode also defaults to `workspace-write`, which denies its file tools outside the working directory but not Bash; `read-only` is refused)\n.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-presentation.txt:222:      C) cli-claude-code — `claude -p \"PROMPT\" --model X --permission-mode X`, where the permission mode is `acceptEdits` at the default `workspace-write` and `bypassPermissions` with `--sandbox-mode=danger-full-access`. The default cannot run Bash in `-p` mode, so a lineage that must record state needs the flag. Optional --effort and optional `--config-dir=PATH` for CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. No service-tier.\n.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research-presentation.txt:131:   |-- --sandbox-mode=<mode> -> config.executor.sandboxMode (`read-only` / `workspace-write` / `danger-full-access`; default `workspace-write`; cli-opencode also defaults to `workspace-write`, which denies its file tools outside the working directory but not Bash; `read-only` is refused)\n.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research-presentation.txt:195:     C) cli-claude-code — `claude -p \"PROMPT\" --model X --permission-mode X`, where the permission mode is `acceptEdits` at the default `workspace-write` and `bypassPermissions` with `--sandbox-mode=danger-full-access`; the default cannot run Bash in `-p` mode, so a lineage that must record state needs the flag. Optional --eff