{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795202772996,"sessionID":"<redacted>","part":{"partID":"prt_1fa6c6103001g66ILxXEEacdTh","sessionID":"<redacted>","messageID":"msg_1fa6c56180016taLIrpOASkXE2","type":"tool","id":"call_0f9c5179ce6c3176dc0e2696","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Review Iteration 012\n\n## Dispatcher\n\n- Mode: review (deep-review LEAF, inline executor for this detached lineage)\n- Session: `fanout-deepseek-1795201537192-sq1ivw`, generation 1, lineageMode `new`\n- Iteration: 12 of 24\n- Focus: **Frontier node F-1-006: maintainability**\n- Dimension: maintainability\n- Frontier node: F-1-006 (depth 1 of 3), scope `.skilled/commands/deep`, `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references`, `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/references`, `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog`\n- Convergence before dispatch: `CONTINUE` / `noStopCandidate`, stuck 0, consecutive failures 0.\n- Sources read this iteration: `steer.md` (absent), `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-findings-registry.json`, `deep-review-strategy.md`, `frontier-nodes.jsonl`, both presentations, both command docs, `executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts`.\n\n## Files Reviewed\n\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/review.md:111`\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/research.md:118`\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-presentation.txt:142,226`\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research-presentation.txt:131,199`\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/compiled/deep-review.contract.markdown:284,371`\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/compiled/deep-research.contract.markdown:279,350`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/tests/unit/executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts:17-25,145-149`\n\n## Findings - New\n\n### P0 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P1 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P2 Findings\n\n1. **The executor help text is hand-maintained in four files and the only test that reads them checks a single rejected flag** -- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-presentation.txt:142` -- The `--sandbox-mode` row and the per-kind help lines are duplicated in `review.md`, `research.md`, `deep-review-presentation.txt` and `deep-research-presentation.txt`, and the two compiled contracts are generated from them. This range had to edit all four to describe the opencode workspace-write default, and the pi line still drifted (R4-P2-001). The parity test that reads the presentations and protocol files (`executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts:145-149`) asserts only that they do not carry the rejected `--pure` flag; nothing compares the sandbox row or the per-kind lines across the four copies.\n   - Finding class: cross-consumer\n   - Scope proof: `rg -l \"cli-opencode also defaults to .workspace-write.\" .skilled/commands/deep .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop` returns the two presentations and the two compiled contracts; `rg -n \"OPENCODE_COMMAND_DOCS\" executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts` shows the list is used only by the `--pure` assertion. The command docs carry a differently worded copy of the same claim, so the four hand-maintained copies are not byte-identical and no test could compare them without a normalized source.\n   - Affected surface hints: [\"deep-review-presentation.txt\", \"deep-research-presentation.txt\", \"review.md\", \"research.md\", \"executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts\"]\n   - Case: a maintainer changes the opencode sandbox behavior and edits the two presentations, missing `review.md` and `research.md`. The compiled contracts regenerate from the presentations and look correct, the `--pure` test passes, and the command docs keep telling operators the old behavior until someone reads both.\n\n## Traceability Checks\n\n| Protocol | Status | Evidence | Summary |\n|----------|--------|----------|---------|\n| `spec_code` | partial | `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/tests/unit/executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts:145-149` | The docs match the code today, but the duplication has no parity guard beyond one negative flag check. |\n| `checklist_evidence` | notApplicable | - | No spec checklist for a commit-range target. |\n| `skill_agent` | notApplicable | - | No agent definition in the range. |\n| `agent_cross_runtime` | notApplicable | - | No runtime mirror in the range. |\n| `feature_catalog_code` | pending | - | Covered in iteration 9; not re-run. |\n| `playbook_capability` | pending | - | Covered in iteration 6; not re-run. |\n\n## Integration Evidence\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/tests/unit/executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts:17-25` names the presentations and both `loop-protocol.md` files as \"the cli-opencode command an operator copies\", so the test's author already treated them as a parity surface; the assertion at `:145-149` covers only `--pure`.\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/compiled/deep-review.contract.markdown:284,371` and the research counterpart carry the generated copies, so a maintainer who edits only the presentations produces a self-consistent generated pair that still disagrees with the command docs.\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/review.md:111` and `.skilled/commands/deep/research.md:118` carry a differently worded copy of the same claim, which is why a byte comparison would not work and a normalized assertion is needed.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- **The four copies are not identical by design.** The command docs use a longer sentence; the presentations use a shorter row. A parity test would need to assert the shared facts (default mode, deny scope, read-only refusal), not the bytes.\n- **The compiled contracts are generated, so they are not a fifth hand-maintained copy.** They inherit whatever the presentations say, which is why the drift risk is in the four sources.\n- **The pi line is the proof of cost.** R4-P2-001 is exactly the drift this duplication permits, and it survived a range whose author edited all four files for the opencode change.\n\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\n\n- **Compiled contracts are generated.** Their digests match their sources (iteration 9), so they cannot drift independently.\n- **The `--pure` guard works.** No doc carries the rejected flag.\n- **The presentations and command docs agree today.** The opencode paragraph and the pi line were compared in iterations 4 and 6; the only disagreement is the pi line already reported.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- **A finding for the compiled contracts.** Ruled out: they are generated and their digests match.\n- **A duplicate of R4-P2-001.** Ruled out: R4-P2-001 is the pi content drift; this finding is the structural duplication that let it happen, in a different file set.\n- **A finding for the `--pure` guard being too narrow on its own.** Ruled out as the same finding: the narrow guard is the evidence, not a separate defect.\n\n## Next Focus\n\n- Dimension: correctness\n- Frontier node: F-2-007 (open, zero visits; the only unvisited node)\n- Focus area: the sandbox enforcement label matrix across every executor kind and all three sandbox modes — whether `sandboxEnforcementFor` reports `preventive`, `compensating` or `none` correctly for each kind, including the kinds whose builder emits a different mode than the one requested.\n- Reason: all three depth-1 nodes are answered or on their last dimension; the admitted child is the remaining frontier work and its question is the one R1-P2-001 opened.\n- Rotation status: F-1-006 all four dimensions covered; F-2-007 correctness next.\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive (test-to-doc comparison found the unguarded duplication); no blocked approaches.\n- Required evidence: `sandboxEnforcementFor`, every builder's `confines` and `compensatingControl` argument, and the conformance test's per-surface assertions.\n- Recovery note: none.\n\nReview verdict: PASS\n","path":"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-012.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-012.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-012.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Review Iteration 012\\n\\n## Dispatcher\\n\\n- Mode: review (deep-review LEAF, inline executor for this detached lineage)\\n- Session: `fanout-deepseek-1795201537192-sq1ivw`, generation 1, lineageMode `new`\\n- Iteration: 12 of 24\\n- Focus: **Frontier node F-1-006: maintainability**\\n- Dimension: maintainability\\n- Frontier node: F-1-006 (depth 1 of 3), scope `.skilled/commands/deep`, `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references`, `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/references`, `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/feature-catalog`\\n- Convergence before dispatch: `CONTINUE` / `noStopCandidate`, stuck 0, consecutive failures 0.\\n- Sources read this iteration: `steer.md` (absent), `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-findings-registry.json`, `deep-review-strategy.md`, `frontier-nodes.jsonl`, both presentations, both command docs, `executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts`.\\n\\n## Files Reviewed\\n\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/review.md:111`\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/research.md:118`\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-presentation.txt:142,226`\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research-presentation.txt:131,199`\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/compiled/deep-review.contract.markdown:284,371`\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/compiled/deep-research.contract.markdown:279,350`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/tests/unit/executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts:17-25,145-149`\\n\\n## Findings - New\\n\\n### P0 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P1 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P2 Findings\\n\\n1. **The executor help text is hand-maintained in four files and the only test that reads them checks a single rejected flag** -- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-presentation.txt:142` -- The `--sandbox-mode` row and the per-kind help lines are duplicated in `review.md`, `research.md`, `deep-review-presentation.txt` and `deep-research-presentation.txt`, and the two compiled contracts are generated from them. This range had to edit all four to describe the opencode workspace-write default, and the pi line still drifted (R4-P2-001). The parity test that reads the presentations and protocol files (`executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts:145-149`) asserts only that they do not carry the rejected `--pure` flag; nothing compares the sandbox row or the per-kind lines across the four copies.\\n   - Finding class: cross-consumer\\n   - Scope proof: `rg -l \\\"cli-opencode also defaults to .workspace-write.\\\" .skilled/commands/deep .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop` returns the two presentations and the two compiled contracts; `rg -n \\\"OPENCODE_COMMAND_DOCS\\\" executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts` shows the list is used only by the `--pure` assertion. The command docs carry a differently worded copy of the same claim, so the four hand-maintained copies are not byte-identical and no test could compare them without a normalized source.\\n   - Affected surface hints: [\\\"deep-review-presentation.txt\\\", \\\"deep-research-presentation.txt\\\", \\\"review.md\\\", \\\"research.md\\\", \\\"executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts\\\"]\\n   - Case: a maintainer changes the opencode sandbox behavior and edits the two presentations, missing `review.md` and `research.md`. The compiled contracts regenerate from the presentations and look correct, the `--pure` test passes, and the command docs keep telling operators the old behavior until someone reads both.\\n\\n## Traceability Checks\\n\\n| Protocol | Status | Evidence | Summary |\\n|----------|--------|----------|---------|\\n| `spec_code` | partial | `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/tests/unit/executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts:145-149` | The docs match the code today, but the duplication has no parity guard beyond one negative flag check. |\\n| `checklist_evidence` | notApplicable | - | No spec checklist for a commit-range target. |\\n| `skill_agent` | notApplicable | - | No agent definition in the range. |\\n| `agent_cross_runtime` | notApplicable | - | No runtime mirror in the range. |\\n| `feature_catalog_code` | pending | - | Covered in iteration 9; not re-run. |\\n| `playbook_capability` | pending | - | Covered in iteration 6; not re-run. |\\n\\n## Integration Evidence\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/tests/unit/executor-branch-contract.vitest.ts:17-25` names the presentations and both `loop-protocol.md` files as \\\"the cli-opencode command an operator copies\\\", so the test's author already treated them as a parity surface; the assertion at `:145-149` covers only `--pure`.\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/compiled/deep-review.contract.markdown:284,371` and the research counterpart carry the generated copies, so a maintainer who edits only the presentations produces a self-consistent generated pair that still disagrees with the command docs.\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/review.md:111` and `.skilled/commands/deep/research.md:118` carry a differently worded copy of the same claim, which is why a byte comparison would not work and a normalized assertion is needed.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- **The four copies are not identical by design.** The command docs use a longer sentence; the presentations use a shorter row. A parity test would need to assert the shared facts (default mode, deny scope, read-only refusal), not the bytes.\\n- **The compiled contracts are generated, so they are not a fifth hand-maintained copy.** They inherit whatever the presentations say, which is why the drift risk is in the four sources.\\n- **The pi line is the proof of cost.** R4-P2-001 is exactly the drift this duplication permits, and it survived a range whose author edited all four files for the opencode change.\\n\\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\\n\\n- **Compiled contracts are generated.** Their digests match their sources (iteration 9), so they cannot drift independently.\\n- **The `--pure` guard works.** No doc carries the rejected flag.\\n- **The presentations and command docs agree today.** The opencode paragraph and the pi line were compared in iterations 4 and 6; the only disagreement is the pi line already reported.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- **A finding for the compiled contracts.** Ruled out: they are generated and their digests match.\\n- **A duplicate of R4-P2-001.** Ruled out: R4-P2-001 is the pi content drift; this finding is the structural duplication that let it happen, in a different file set.\\n- **A finding for the `--pure` guard being too narrow on its own.** Ruled out as the same finding: the narrow guard is the evidence, not a separate defect.\\n\\n## Next Focus\\n\\n- Dimension: correctness\\n- Frontier node: F-2-007 (open, zero visits; the only unvisited node)\\n- Focus area: the sandbox enforcement label matrix across every executor kind and all three sandbox modes — whether `sandboxEnforcementFor` reports `preventive`, `compensating` or `none` correctly for each kind, including the kinds whose builder emits a different mode than the one requested.\\n- Reason: all three depth-1 nodes are answered or on their last dimension; the admitted child is the remaining frontier work and its question is the one R1-P2-001 opened.\\n- Rotation status: F-1-006 all four dimensions covered; F-2-007 correctness next.\\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive (test-to-doc comparison found the unguarded duplication); no blocked approaches.\\n- Required evidence: `sandboxEnforcementFor`, every builder's `confines` and `compensatingControl` argument, and the conformance test's per-surface assertions.\\n- Recovery note: none.\\n\\nReview verdict: PASS\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-012.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795202769155,"end":1795202772996}}}}
